issues.body:
Found while reviewing the base-move carryover (#80) on 23 Oct 2026. GitHub's tree lists a symlink as a blob with mode `120000` whose sha and size are those of the link text (the target path), while the contents API serves the target file's content. For a repository skill under `.github/skills`, `.agents/skills` or `.claude/skills` whose `SKILL.md` or resource is a symlink:

- the skill caps in `src/repository/skills.ts` read the listed size, so a symlink to a 200 KiB file passes the 50 KiB file cap and the 512 KiB base cap, and the served body can exceed what the caps promised;
- the `repo@<8>` fingerprint hashes `path:sha` pairs, so a change to the target changes nothing in the fingerprint, and a review after the change still claims the old skills build;
- the catalog serves the target's content as the skill's body, which is what a repository author expects, so refusing symlinks outright would surprise them.

The tree entries now carry `mode` (since the carryover change), so the fix can treat a `120000` entry explicitly: either resolve it to its target's listing entry (same base, by path) for size and sha before the caps and the fingerprint, or leave symlinked skill files out of the catalog with a `skills.capped`-style log naming the reason. The instruction-file path has the same shape but no caps beyond the 12,000-character render cap, which measures the served text, so it is unaffected.
